# Types and single responsibility (Go)

Do **not** use monolithic `types.go` files that accumulate unrelated structs.

## Rule

- Put each **primary exported type** (struct, interface, or type alias) in its
  own file when the type carries a distinct domain or wire contract.
- Include **closely related** helpers in the same file (e.g. `PullRequest` and
  `EncodePullRequest`, or a small interface and its sole implementation when
  they are inseparable).
- A package may keep a thin **`interfaces.go`** for a small set of shared
  interfaces — not a dumping ground for every struct in the service.
- Avoid **`types.go`** as a catch-all; prefer names that match the type
  (`pullrequest.go`, `trust_root_client.go`).

## File naming

- Name the file after the primary type in **snake_case** (Go convention).
- Multi-word domain names use underscores (`genesis_doc.go`, `grant_chain.go`).

## Imports

- Prefer importing concrete packages; keep type definitions near the code that
  owns the contract (HTTP handler, queue consumer, KMS signer).
- When splitting an overloaded file, move one type at a time and update tests
  in the same change.
